Here’s a clear explanation of the difference between hard links and soft (symbolic) links in Linux 👇
Feature	Hard Link	Soft Link (Symbolic Link)
Definition	A hard link is a direct reference to the same physical data (inode) on the disk.	A soft link is a shortcut or pointer that refers to the original file’s pathname.
Inode	Shares the same inode number as the original file.	Has a different inode number from the original file.
File Deletion Effect	If the original file is deleted, the data still exists and can be accessed via the hard link.	If the original file is deleted, the soft link becomes broken (it points to a non-existing file).
Cross Filesystem	Cannot link files across different filesystems or partitions.	Can link files across different filesystems or partitions.
Directories	Usually not allowed to create hard links to directories (to prevent loops).	Can link to directories.
Command Example	ln source.txt link.txt	ln -s source.txt link.txt
Example:
# Hard link
ln file1.txt hardlink.txt

# Soft (symbolic) link
ln -s file1.txt softlink.txt
✅ Summary:
•	Hard link → another name for the same file content.
•	Soft link → a shortcut pointing to the file’s path.

5.	A file has permissions -rwxr-x--x. Explain who can read, write, and execute it.
🔹 Structure of Linux File Permissions:
The permission string has 10 characters:
-rwxr-x--x
Position	Meaning	Who it applies to	Permission
1	-	File type (- = regular file)	—
2–4	rwx	Owner (User)	Read ✅, Write ✅, Execute ✅
5–7	r-x	Group	Read ✅, Execute ✅, Write ❌
8–10	--x	Others (World)	Execute ✅ only
🔹 Interpretation:
User Category	Read	Write	Execute
Owner	✅	✅	✅
Group	✅	❌	✅
Others	❌	❌	✅
✅ Summary:
•	Owner can read, write, and execute the file.
•	Group can read and execute, but not write.
•	Others can only execute the file.

