8.	What is the difference between apropos (i.e., man -k) and whatis (i.e., man -f)?
Linux:
Feature	apropos (man -k)	whatis (man -f)
Purpose	Searches the manual page descriptions for a keyword or phrase.	Displays a short one-line description of a specific command.
Search Type	Partial or keyword match; can return multiple results.	Exact match; usually returns a single result.
Usage	apropos keyword or man -k keyword	whatis command or man -f command
Output Example	apropos copy → may show: cp (1) - copy files and directories
scp (1) - secure copy (remote file copy program)	whatis cp → cp (1) - copy files and directories
When to Use	When you don’t know the exact command but know the functionality.	When you know the command and just want a brief description.

9.	Write a command to redirect the error output of a command to a file named error.log.
In Linux, to redirect only the error output (stderr) of a command to a file named error.log, you can use:
command 2> error.log
🔹 Explanation:
•	command → the command you are running.
•	2> → redirects file descriptor 2, which is stderr (error messages).
•	error.log → the file where errors will be saved.
 
🔹 Example:
ls /nonexistentfolder 2> error.log
•	Since the folder doesn’t exist, the error message will be written to error.log instead of appearing on the screen.
